--TEST--
`Core\Uuid::v7`'s millisecond field never goes backwards across a sweep of consecutive draws — counted, contrasted against `v4`'s leading field, and bounded on both sides as a real Unix-epoch clock — spec § 11
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A drawn UUID cannot be asserted, so v7's one behavioural claim — that it is
// *time-ordered*, which is the whole reason to prefer it over v4 for a database
// key — is pinned by counting a run of draws rather than by comparing two.
// `uuid-every-draw-round-trips-and-a-refusal-quotes-boundedly.nvst` sweeps the
// shape, the round trip and the distinctness; nothing here repeats them, and
// nothing there looks at the ordering at all.
//
// The field under test is the first twelve hex digits, which RFC 9562 § 5.7
// fixes as a 48-bit big-endian millisecond count. Zero-padded fixed-width hex
// orders the same way the number does, so `Core\Str::compare` bytewise is the
// numeric comparison — and it is the only one available, because `<` over two
// `string`s does not lower. The hyphen sits at index 8 of every rendering, so
// slicing 13 characters rather than splicing the two groups together compares
// the same positions on both sides.
int $ordered = 0;
int $i = 0;
var $prev = Core\Str::slice(Core\Uuid::v7()->toString(), 0, 13);
var $first = $prev;
for ($i = 0; $i < 64; $i = $i + 1) {
    var $next = Core\Str::slice(Core\Uuid::v7()->toString(), 0, 13);
    $ordered = $ordered + ((Core\Str::compare($prev, $next) <= 0) ? 1 : 0);
    $prev = $next;
}
echo "v7 ordered ", $ordered, " of 64\n";

// Adjacent pairs holding is not the same claim as the run holding: a clock read
// per draw could step forward 63 times and once backwards past its own start.
// So the last stamp is compared against the first as well.
echo "span ", (Core\Str::compare($first, $prev) <= 0) ? "monotone" : "backwards", "\n";

// The same sweep over v4, whose leading field is 48 random bits. Its count is
// what shows the counter above can say no: 65 uniform draws arriving already
// sorted is a 1-in-65! event, so this line is an assertion and not a hope.
int $scattered = 0;
var $before = Core\Str::slice(Core\Uuid::v4()->toString(), 0, 13);
for ($i = 0; $i < 64; $i = $i + 1) {
    var $after = Core\Str::slice(Core\Uuid::v4()->toString(), 0, 13);
    $scattered = $scattered + ((Core\Str::compare($before, $after) <= 0) ? 1 : 0);
    $before = $after;
}
echo "v4 ", ($scattered < 64) ? "unordered" : "ordered", "\n";

// Ordering alone would still hold for a counter that never restarts, so the
// field is bounded on both sides against two dates written as the milliseconds
// they are: `016f5e66-e800` is 2020-01-01 and `03bb2cc3-d800` is 2100-01-01.
// That is what makes the leading bits the *clock* this module's doc says they
// are — the one `Core\Time::now()` reads, since both go through `jiff` — rather
// than a monotonic sequence of their own. The sibling case asserts two digits
// of this prefix; these are the twelve it belongs to.
var $now = Core\Str::slice(Core\Uuid::v7()->toString(), 0, 13);
echo "clock ",
     (Core\Str::compare("016f5e66-e800", $now) < 0) ? "after-2020" : "before-2020",
     " ",
     (Core\Str::compare($now, "03bb2cc3-d800") < 0) ? "before-2100" : "after-2100",
     "\n";
--EXPECT--
v7 ordered 64 of 64
span monotone
v4 unordered
clock after-2020 before-2100
